    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study of 3D joints propagation between interfaces uses a boundary element code based on a solution for the angular dislocation. The variation of KI along the tip line is studied for joints initiating in the middle of the layer before abutting with the interface and during their propagation in the layer. The interfaces have no friction. The effects of these slipping interfaces on KI are numerically evaluated and are interpreted in terms of a \"preferential\" shape toward which the joint is likely to propagate. The ellipsoid joint confined between two interfaces is likely to propagate preferentially toward a penny-crack shape unless the major axis is perpendicular to layering. Joints with circular shapes imply slipping interfaces whereas joints with elliptical shapes imply frictional interfaces. The spacing of the rib marks observed on joints close to interfaces could be an indication of the influence of those interfaces. Once the joint abuts on the interfaces without crossing them as they slip, the joint tip line in the direction of the layer tends to be straight. As the joint continues to propagate, this front becomes rounded. These results show how the effect of slipping interfaces can be taken into account when interpreting or modeling joint propagation.",
